WEBVTT

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.090
Plant the explosives in the ship's hold.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.490
Twice the standard amount.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.610
What about the ship's crew?

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.450
The crew?

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.300
Hey!

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:18.410
Let us out!

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.250
As our information said, 
they had the instructions.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.640
Don't leave a shred of evidence.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:31.730
Don't!

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.010
<i>As the embers of world war smoldered</i>

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.810
<i>in autumn of 1937,</i>

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.100
<i>the Imperial Army</i>

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.100
<b>Greater East Asia 
Cultural Society</b>

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.390
<i>established a clandestine 
spy training agency.</i>

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.730
<b>Greater East Asia 
Cultural Society</b>

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.060
<i>The histories, names, and even ages</i>

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.570
<i>of the elite men who pass its 
rigorous application process</i>

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.860
<i>are all treated as top-secret information.</i>

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.580
<i>They easily complete training 
exercises that push them</i>

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.760
<i>to their physical and mental limits.</i>

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.250
<i>Under the command of founder 
Lieutenant Colonel Yuuki,</i>

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.830
<i>they operate worldwide, within the shadows.</i>

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.700
<i>And so a brand-new intelligence 
agency was created.</i>

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.910
<i>Its name is "D-Agency."</i>

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.500
There it is!

00:02:54.570 --> 00:03:01.070
Episode 7 - Codename: Cerberus

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:57.280
The islands!

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:58.860
Finally.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.350
The Hawaiian Islands.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:05.910
We should be docking in 
Honolulu in about three hours.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:15.340
This program is a work of fiction. 
The characters, organizations, locations, laws, names, 
etc. being portrayed are unrelated to actual events.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.270
You're not going to see the view?

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:17.140
Everyone's gone over to the starboard deck...

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:20.630
I'm a little preoccupied right now.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.610
A crossword puzzle?

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.510
"The god of the ocean."

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:26.750
Pardon?

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.130
Eight letters, and the third is "S."

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:30.320
Do you know it?

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:33.660
Poseidon.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.520
Ask a man of the sea about the sea, huh?

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.530
How about this one?

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.680
"Blank" Variations, six letters.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.340
I think the last letter is "A."

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.150
I'm sorry, I'm afraid I'm not sure...

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:57.030
All right, I'll skip this vertical 
one for now. Next is...

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.490
No! Stop!

00:03:59.140 --> 00:03:59.920
Stay!

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.000
Is something wrong?

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.870
A dog came running from out 
of nowhere and startled her.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:18.070
Here, come with me.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.490
Look. Dolphins.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:36.930
Mommy, dolphins!

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.800
Come on, we'll go see them.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:40.680
Okay!

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:50.870
What's your name?

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:51.740
Emma!

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.540
Ah, that's a nice name!

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:54.390
Will you be returning to your room?

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.850
No, I'll be fine once I rest a bit.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.820
One, two, three, four,

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:02.180
five dolphins!

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:06.140
They went away...

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.060
Where'd they go?

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.230
Back to their houses, maybe?

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.360
Where are the dolphins' houses?

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.050
Who knows?

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:19.930
Thank you very much.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.960
I feel much better.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:24.670
Come along, Emma.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:27.990
What do you say?

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.090
Thank you!

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:31.120
You're welcome.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.080
Can I help you?

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.080
Oh, pardon me.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.580
I was passing by and couldn't 
help but notice this.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.450
And before I knew it...

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.640
Jeffrey Morgan.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:59.660
I run a small trading company in San Francisco.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.380
Osamu Utsumi. I'm an engineer from Japan.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.040
It's not in my nature to

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.550
walk away from a crossword puzzle.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.090
One of my bad habits.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.060
That's perfect!

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.280
Can I borrow that knowledge for a bit?

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.310
I'm actually completely stumped right now.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.450
"A marshy region on the 
shore of the Baltic Sea."

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.660
The last word was Poseidon, 
so I know the first letter is "P."

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:26.380
Pomorze.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:29.550
I see! Pomorze...

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:32.730
How about this one?

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.320
"Blank" Variations, six letters.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.120
I think the last letter is "A."

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.760
Enigma.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:47.360
Enigma... Variations?

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:51.550
One of the English composer 
Elgar's masterpieces.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.860
It's Greek for "mystery."

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.100
Elgar wrote several riddles into the piece,

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:00.180
some of which still haven't 
been solved to this day.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.850
Ah, an unsolvable mystery.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.560
It's the same thing.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.320
The same thing? As what?

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:12.210
The Enigma machine that the Germans are using.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:15.780
Impenetrable. Invincible.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.520
A mystery that can never be solved.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.400
My company obtained one

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.170
that had been exhibited at 
the Postal Union Congress

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:29.820
in Leipzig, and used it to its fullest.

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.060
Then, all of a sudden, they all 
disappeared from the market,

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.060
and when I looked into it, I learned 
it was being used by the Germans.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.810
I've heard a bit about that myself.

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:46.000
After that, the Germans supposedly 
modified Enigma machines

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.850
to make them capable of using 
over 200 trillion combinations.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.040
I see.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.470
So the movements of German U-boats 
that had Enigma-encrypted orders

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:59.470
would be completely unpredictable.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:02.470
I wonder about that.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:07.480
Logically speaking, any form of

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.690
man-made encryption can be broken.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:14.180
You just need a little hint.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:15.840
Such as?

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.520
Let's say, hypothetically,

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:22.310
you have a message, the contents
of which you already know.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.030
If you had an Enigma-encrypted 
version of the same message,

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:29.030
comparing them would give you a clue

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:31.190
as to how to decrypt them.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:32.830
Makes sense.

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.860
You really are a smart man.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.910
What's so funny?

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:39.490
My apologies.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:40.980
As you know,

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.800
the Japanese Navy is developing 
encryption machines

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.630
based on the same technology as the Enigma.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.550
We can't have someone like 
you slip into our country

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.400
by pretending to be an American.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.800
Wh-What...

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:58.810
Time to drop the act,

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:00.720
Mr. Louis McCloud.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:02.220
Or...

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:05.870
By your country's intelligence 
agency's codename,

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:08.740
should I call you "The Prof"?

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.180
So you're Cerberus...

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:30.590
You don't need a knife to 
sever the carotid artery.

00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:32.040
Fingernails are enough.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:50.750
How?

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:53.470
How did you know who I was?

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.710
With all this plastic surgery,

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:58.630
even my oldest friends

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:01.750
or closest family wouldn't recognize me.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:03.830
I only had a few bits

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:06.460
of random intel on you.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:09.400
However, combining what I had

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:12.040
brought a few of your idiosyncrasies to light.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:16.920
For example, an inability to 
ignore crossword puzzles.

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:22.060
Of course, the clues were 
tailored to you, as well.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:23.820
Enigma...

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.860
Then the crossword puzzle

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.390
was a test to confirm my identity?

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.770
To be honest, I knew it was you

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.940
from the moment I saw you.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:39.420
Several of your facial features 
matched the picture I had.

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:41.880
Like fingerprints, there are

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:44.120
some things unique to each individual.

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:45.760
Features?

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:47.060
What—

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:47.840
A ship!

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:49.560
A ship's approaching!

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.250
Is it the Japanese Navy?

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:56.420
No, that's not Japanese...

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:04.610
Oh my God!

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:05.430
That's...

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:24.200
<b>"Stop your vessel at once."</b>

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:28.320
My, oh my, to think my 
Royal Navy would show up here!

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:32.470
That's the very definition of "unpredictable."

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.680
Well, I certainly wasn't bored, thanks to you.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:38.340
I even got to solve a crossword puzzle.

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:42.590
Utsumi, this is goodbye.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:45.130
Cheers.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:02.410
So...

00:12:02.410 --> 00:12:05.040
You are... Cerb...

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:18.880
<b>Greater East Asia
Cultural Society</b>

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:27.080
Louis McCloud.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:30.040
During the last war in Europe,

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:32.350
he was hired by British Secret Intelligence

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:35.050
and worked on decrypting 
German communications.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:37.720
Now, he's changed his face

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:40.940
and plans to travel across the 
Pacific from America to Japan.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:44.290
Do not let him set foot in Japan.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:51.640
The Gardening Plan...

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:02.300
Utsumi-sama.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:05.690
Are you saying you know nothing

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:09.570
about the late Mr. Morgan, beyond his name?

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.900
I just met him on the ship, I'm afraid.

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:13.850
Nothing strange about that, is there?

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:15.900
Do they know the cause of death?

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:19.550
The ship's on-board doctor 
wrote in the autopsy...

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:21.080
"Cause of death...

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:24.150
is cyanide poisoning."

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:25.980
He was poisoned?!

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:28.160
Well, it's not certain yet.

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:30.570
So...

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:31.990
You are...

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:34.150
Cerb...

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:42.320
<i>So McCloud, a key member of 
their Enigma decryption team,</i>

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:47.300
<i>was being targeted by someone 
with the codename Cerberus,</i>

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:48.890
<i>the guard dog of Hades.</i>

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.430
As the captain of this ship, I object to this.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:55.510
You're violating international law.

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.890
I demand an explanation!

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.540
We've been informed that an 
Englishman, Jeffrey Morgan,

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.750
is being held prisoner on a stateless vessel.

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:04.860
An Englishman?!

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:08.740
We've consulted the American authorities,

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:12.350
and are conducting unannounced 
investigations of suspicious ships.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:16.080
There won't be trouble once his safety 
is confirmed and he's returned to us.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:18.250
Now, where is he?

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:20.930
He's...

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:22.530
Well?

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.440
If you refuse to hand him 
over, that's fine, too.

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.170
Are you feeling all right, Mrs. Grane?

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:28.170
We'll just search the ship ourselves.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:29.900
Yes, thanks to you.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:31.340
That's ludicrous! This is a 
Japanese passenger ship!

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:31.340
Glad to hear it.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:47.870
What?

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:49.720
Dead?!

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:52.650
That's impossible! What—

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:55.370
Come on, Frate!

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:56.700
Come here!

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:59.050
Utsumi-sama, what are you doing?!

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:11.970
I can explain Mr. Morgan's death.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:15.640
However, may I have a little time first?

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.310
Is that all right?

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:22.380
Fine, as long as it gets me
a satisfactory answer.

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:35.310
You're Cerberus, yes?

00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:42.710
Frate here isn't that unlike 
the guard dog of Hades.

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:45.860
Can you explain this?

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:53.170
How did you know it was him?

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:55.930
I knew the moment I laid eyes on him.

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:57.750
I stare a hole

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:00.500
through that photo every day.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:04.600
I'd heard McCloud reconstructed his face.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:08.640
But no amount of facial 
surgery changes the ears,

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:10.530
so I focused on them the most.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.100
Emma took ill after we left port,

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:15.960
and when she finally felt better,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.160
I started looking for the ears in the photo.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.160
But at that moment,

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:25.000
just as I'd finally found McCloud,

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.810
I saw Frate at his feet, and I panicked.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:32.750
Because that photo was 
hidden inside his collar.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:34.720
No! Stop!

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:35.980
Stay!

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:41.720
That's why you shouted at him.

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:45.480
But you've already accomplished your task, no?

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:48.520
If you threw that photo into the ocean,

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:51.110
there'd be no physical evidence left.

00:16:55.180 --> 00:16:59.580
This is the most handsome 
photo of Raymond I have.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.900
I can't throw it away, no matter how ugly

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:05.830
the killer standing next to him is.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:09.780
He was my husband, and her father.

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:11.810
And this man

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:14.460
was an MI5 spy.

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:17.360
He stole my husband,

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:19.740
her father, from us.

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:24.420
Now I've killed him, and avenged my husband.

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:26.560
I have no regrets.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:32.880
About a year ago, a German merchant raider

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:34.760
sunk an English cargo ship.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:38.530
The first mate on that ship was a Raymond Grane.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.420
Take heart, Synthia.

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:50.520
I know. I'm okay.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:54.700
Raymond carried out his 
duty as an English seaman.

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:56.380
He was a fine man.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:58.120
Yes...

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:00.200
Emma?

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:02.900
Excuse me.

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:08.260
Emma?

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:10.270
Where did you go, Emma?

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:21.580
Emma...

00:18:28.570 --> 00:18:29.800
It's too cruel!

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:31.140
Stop, you're too loud.

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:34.600
I won't stand for strategies like this.

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.190
Using civilian ships as decoys...

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:42.310
You used a double agent to leak 
that information on purpose!

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:45.720
And you knew what'd happen...

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:50.510
You knew the Germans would eradicate 
the ship to destroy all the evidence!

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:52.950
If we can't break Enigma's encryption,

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:54.690
England will never win this war.

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:58.000
And this plan I devised is guaranteed

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:01.160
to shatter the unbreakable Enigma.

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:04.080
I'll plant the seed, and harvest it myself.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:05.700
I call it...

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:08.420
"Gardening"...

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:10.380
That's what

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:13.600
McCloud called the plan.

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:19.780
The German Navy used Enigma to encrypt 
the contents of the stolen orders

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:22.350
and telegraphed it to friendly troops.

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:26.790
The English intercepted the transmission, 
and compared the strategic plans within

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:29.060
to the Enigma-encrypted document

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:31.210
to find a clue as to how to decrypt it.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.840
However, since he carried out the plan on his own,

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:41.880
McCloud was instead met with antipathy 
from MI5 and they disavowed him.

00:19:42.410 --> 00:19:44.810
He must've changed his face and left for Japan

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:47.720
to find a new place for himself.

00:19:48.230 --> 00:19:49.380
Who...

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:51.330
are you?

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:56.080
You live your life shrouded 
in mystery, too, don't you?

00:19:58.090 --> 00:19:59.090
I became

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:02.350
a German spy in order to get my revenge.

00:20:03.380 --> 00:20:05.050
I'm prepared for the consequences.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:08.960
But when I look at her face,

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:10.520
I don't know anymore...

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:12.810
What I lived for,

00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:16.530
or what I should have lived for...

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:20.990
Mommy?

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:22.530
What's wrong?

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:29.450
Emma, want to see the dolphins again?

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:33.390
Yeah!

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:52.170
Come, Frate!

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:04.930
Goodbye, Emma.

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:21.900
Are the dolphins taking a nap?

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:23.980
We'll see them tomorrow.

00:21:23.980 --> 00:21:26.270
Yeah, with Mommy!

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:29.550
Don't worry. You can come, too.

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:36.420
<i>The two of us and a dog, huh?</i>

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:40.640
<i>Oh, well... It'll work out somehow.</i>

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:22.670
<b>Preview</b>

00:23:23.290 --> 00:23:25.840
That's quite a tough move.

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:28.460
Chess is a sport.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.670
And a violent one, at that.

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:34.880
Episode 8 - Double Joker (Part 1)

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:34.880
Care to play a game?
